WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.584 --> 00:00:09.673
Our lives are made up of small moments
and small decisions.

00:00:11.216 --> 00:00:12.551
Left or right,

00:00:12.551 --> 00:00:14.135
walk or drive,

00:00:14.135 --> 00:00:16.680
eat in or take out,

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.473
lend a helping hand,

00:00:18.473 --> 00:00:20.433
or some words of encouragement

00:00:20.475 --> 00:00:23.979
or an offer of support to a neighbor,

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:26.106
a colleague, a stranger.

00:00:27.524 --> 00:00:30.318
Some of these decisions
are not like the others.

00:00:31.987 --> 00:00:33.780
And with the world in this much pain,

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:38.910
it might seem futile to be focusing
on those kinds of micro decisions.

00:00:39.828 --> 00:00:41.204
But the truth is

00:00:41.204 --> 00:00:46.001
that we underestimate the power
of our own generous actions.

00:00:47.377 --> 00:00:52.382
Every time we intentionally
choose generosity,

00:00:52.382 --> 00:00:56.344
the effects of that choice
are more powerful

00:00:56.386 --> 00:00:59.264
and more far-reaching than we might think.

00:00:59.598 --> 00:01:03.893
The truth is that what one person
can accomplish is enormous.

00:01:05.729 --> 00:01:08.315
I've been one of the many leaders,

00:01:08.356 --> 00:01:09.858
many of them here in this room,

00:01:09.858 --> 00:01:13.737
of Giving Tuesday since
it was created in 2012.

00:01:14.321 --> 00:01:16.323
And it began as an experiment.

00:01:17.324 --> 00:01:21.453
Could we use social media
to create a day of giving,

00:01:21.494 --> 00:01:23.204
following two days of consumption,

00:01:23.246 --> 00:01:25.874
Black Friday and Cyber Monday,
here in the US?

00:01:25.915 --> 00:01:28.710
And if we could, would anybody
be interested in it?

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:31.421
Well, needless to say, the answer was yes.

00:01:31.755 --> 00:01:34.966
But I think that even all of us
who were there at the very beginning

00:01:35.008 --> 00:01:40.930
were genuinely stunned
by just how deeply and widely

00:01:40.972 --> 00:01:43.308
the idea resonated.

00:01:44.184 --> 00:01:48.521
Nonprofits and families and schools
and houses of worship

00:01:48.563 --> 00:01:54.069
all joined in with such
enthusiasm and creativity.

00:01:55.362 --> 00:01:58.865
People felt like they
were asked to be givers

00:01:58.907 --> 00:02:02.077
or invited to be givers
for the first time,

00:02:02.118 --> 00:02:04.037
or in a totally new way.

00:02:04.579 --> 00:02:07.957
I will always remember
the woman who posted,

00:02:07.957 --> 00:02:09.125
"I'm not rich,

00:02:09.125 --> 00:02:10.627
I don't have a million dollars,

00:02:10.669 --> 00:02:13.505
but I can help change lives
on Giving Tuesday."

00:02:14.422 --> 00:02:17.258
And with the spark that that ignited,

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:20.345
it spread throughout
the whole United States

00:02:20.345 --> 00:02:23.765
and then throughout the entire world.

00:02:24.516 --> 00:02:26.976
And now, 11 years later,

00:02:27.018 --> 00:02:30.689
Giving Tuesday is a year-round
global generosity movement

00:02:30.689 --> 00:02:33.108
made up of thousands of leaders

00:02:33.108 --> 00:02:35.944
and millions and millions of people

00:02:35.985 --> 00:02:40.156
who are working to transform
their communities and their world,

00:02:40.198 --> 00:02:42.909
one small act at a time.

00:02:44.577 --> 00:02:46.621
And as a global family,

00:02:46.621 --> 00:02:49.374
because that's how
we think about ourselves,

00:02:49.374 --> 00:02:53.545
we look and sound and work and worship

00:02:53.545 --> 00:02:55.213
in very different ways.

00:02:56.339 --> 00:02:59.342
But what we share
and what keeps us together

00:02:59.384 --> 00:03:01.886
and keeps us working, is a dream

00:03:02.721 --> 00:03:06.599
of a future based in the value
of radical generosity.

00:03:08.727 --> 00:03:12.439
That phrase might sound
like it means something big

00:03:12.439 --> 00:03:15.483
or extreme or dramatic or newsworthy.

00:03:16.526 --> 00:03:18.153
Or kind of scary.

00:03:18.194 --> 00:03:20.530
But it's actually the opposite.

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:24.576
The word radical simply
means “from the root.”

00:03:24.576 --> 00:03:27.287
And so we imagine a world

00:03:27.328 --> 00:03:31.833
where generosity is simply
at the root of our decisions

00:03:31.833 --> 00:03:33.501
and our behaviors,

00:03:33.543 --> 00:03:38.006
even, and maybe especially,
down to our smallest

00:03:38.006 --> 00:03:40.008
and most mundane ones.

00:03:42.552 --> 00:03:48.183
I have essentially been on
a decade-long global generosity tour,

00:03:48.183 --> 00:03:52.061
witnessing this principle
of radical generosity in action.

00:03:52.645 --> 00:03:58.568
I have been welcomed and hugged and fed
in more countries than I can count.

00:03:58.943 --> 00:04:03.448
And from Kenya to Colombia to Canada,

00:04:03.448 --> 00:04:05.200
so many more places,

00:04:05.241 --> 00:04:10.538
I have witnessed the hundreds
of ways that generosity manifests.

00:04:11.331 --> 00:04:13.500
And what I have learned,

00:04:13.541 --> 00:04:17.045
my understanding of what generosity is

00:04:17.045 --> 00:04:21.216
and my appreciation
for what it can accomplish,

00:04:21.257 --> 00:04:24.719
has expanded 1,000 times over.

00:04:26.095 --> 00:04:27.722
I've unlearned a lot of lessons,

00:04:27.764 --> 00:04:30.266
and I've learned a lot of lessons
about generosity.

00:04:32.227 --> 00:04:35.647
Often when we think about giving,
we think about money.

00:04:36.856 --> 00:04:40.860
And yes, money is an incredibly
important form of giving.

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:45.573
It is crucial for the health
and sustainability of our civil societies.

00:04:45.615 --> 00:04:49.410
But thinking about giving
only in terms of money

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:51.579
is like thinking about love

00:04:51.621 --> 00:04:53.873
only in terms of diamonds.

00:04:54.916 --> 00:04:58.545
We love what we can measure,
we love what we can quantify.

00:04:59.295 --> 00:05:02.257
But I think what we would love even more

00:05:02.257 --> 00:05:07.303
is to live in a world
of immeasurable abundance,

00:05:07.303 --> 00:05:09.264
empathy and solidarity.

00:05:11.015 --> 00:05:15.311
Generosity builds a bridge
between this world that we live in now

00:05:15.311 --> 00:05:16.479
and that one.

00:05:17.105 --> 00:05:21.109
And it builds bridges between us
at the same time.

00:05:22.443 --> 00:05:24.112
One small act at a time.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:29.534
So what do some
of those small acts look like?

00:05:29.576 --> 00:05:32.370
They might look like in Toronto,

00:05:32.412 --> 00:05:37.250
where people chalked messages
of gratitude and love

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:39.752
on city sidewalks,

00:05:39.752 --> 00:05:43.047
just to uplift the spirits
of anyone who might walk by.

00:05:43.089 --> 00:05:46.175
And then a lot of the people
who did walk by picked up the chalk,

00:05:46.217 --> 00:05:48.344
and they added their own messages.

00:05:48.678 --> 00:05:50.388
This picture happens to be Toronto,

00:05:50.388 --> 00:05:53.933
but this project actually happened
in dozens of cities across the country

00:05:53.975 --> 00:05:55.226
and across the world.

00:05:55.226 --> 00:05:56.644
Or in Nairobi,

00:05:56.686 --> 00:05:59.063
where people painted rocks

00:05:59.105 --> 00:06:03.359
with bright colors
and inspirational messages,

00:06:03.401 --> 00:06:05.403
and they put them all over the city,

00:06:05.403 --> 00:06:07.614
park benches, bus stops,

00:06:07.614 --> 00:06:10.950
just to bring smiles
to the faces of strangers,

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:13.077
smiles that they would never even see.

00:06:13.453 --> 00:06:18.249
And turning the Earth’s literal
most mundane object into a love letter.

00:06:19.918 --> 00:06:24.881
In Jackson, Tennessee, one of our longtime
Giving Tuesday leaders, Allison,

00:06:24.923 --> 00:06:31.387
was inspired to combat food insecurity
in her community in one small way,

00:06:31.429 --> 00:06:34.432
which was building a set
of little free pantries.

00:06:35.058 --> 00:06:38.186
And she put a message
on Facebook asking for help.

00:06:38.227 --> 00:06:43.191
People responded and they built
an initial six boxes,

00:06:43.232 --> 00:06:47.320
which local residents then stocked
with food and other supplies.

00:06:47.695 --> 00:06:51.532
All of this done on donated supplies
and volunteer time.

00:06:53.201 --> 00:06:58.831
And that has now turned into over 40 boxes
over three different counties,

00:06:58.873 --> 00:07:04.712
with a network of hundreds
of volunteers keeping it stocked.

00:07:06.381 --> 00:07:08.883
What these stories have in common,

00:07:08.925 --> 00:07:10.927
some of them are much more elaborate

00:07:10.927 --> 00:07:14.597
and some of them are literally
just a fleeting moment,

00:07:14.639 --> 00:07:16.015
but what they have in common

00:07:16.015 --> 00:07:20.395
is that each one inspired at least
one person who was watching.

00:07:21.521 --> 00:07:25.984
Researchers think that

00:07:26.025 --> 00:07:30.154
for every act of kindness
or good deed a person does

00:07:30.196 --> 00:07:35.326
they can affect someone, or many people,
three or more degrees removed from them.

00:07:35.827 --> 00:07:39.539
Imagine that next time you do
a small good thing for someone,

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:42.750
that you might be inspiring people
you will never meet

00:07:42.792 --> 00:07:44.252
to do good as well.

00:07:44.293 --> 00:07:49.215
Your one fleeting moment
could be amplified 100 times over.

00:07:50.842 --> 00:07:57.015
But researchers also have found
that we tend to drastically underestimate

00:07:57.015 --> 00:07:58.891
the effect of our own actions

00:07:58.891 --> 00:08:03.396
and how much they will be
appreciated by the recipient,

00:08:03.396 --> 00:08:07.775
and that that miscalculation
might hold us back

00:08:07.817 --> 00:08:10.695
from doing nice things
for others more often.

00:08:11.988 --> 00:08:15.908
We also think, often, that it's great,

00:08:15.908 --> 00:08:18.619
like, let's do more nice things
for more people, yes.

00:08:18.619 --> 00:08:23.583
But also, "my life is stressful
and hectic and hard,

00:08:23.624 --> 00:08:27.086
and that takes up time
that I might not necessarily have."

00:08:28.129 --> 00:08:31.090
I find myself in that thought
process all the time,

00:08:31.132 --> 00:08:37.638
and what I remind myself of
is that generosity alleviates stress.

00:08:38.181 --> 00:08:39.640
It doesn't add to it.

00:08:40.266 --> 00:08:44.896
People who give are healthier
and happier and more hopeful.

00:08:45.313 --> 00:08:47.148
Just the act of paying for coffee

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:50.610
for the person behind us
in line in the morning

00:08:50.651 --> 00:08:55.656
measurably enhances our feeling
of well-being for the entire day.

00:08:58.993 --> 00:09:00.161
This is Sujan.

00:09:01.996 --> 00:09:06.209
He is a Nepalese restaurateur in London.

00:09:07.043 --> 00:09:10.630
And Sujan has done
a world of good in his life.

00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:12.423
But during the pandemic,

00:09:12.423 --> 00:09:14.175
he started doing what he does best,

00:09:14.217 --> 00:09:20.640
and he began to cook hot, free meals
for overwhelmed hospital workers.

00:09:21.182 --> 00:09:25.895
And when he was starting to do that,
other people saw, they were inspired,

00:09:25.937 --> 00:09:27.980
they asked if they
could join and help out.

00:09:28.481 --> 00:09:31.818
And soon they were feeding
ambulance crews,

00:09:31.818 --> 00:09:34.987
fire crews, charity workers.

00:09:34.987 --> 00:09:40.159
And one year after Sujan started cooking
these delicious hot, free meals,

00:09:40.201 --> 00:09:44.163
he delivered his 100,000th free meal.

00:09:45.039 --> 00:09:49.335
Now, Sujan closes
his restaurant every Tuesday,

00:09:49.377 --> 00:09:51.129
every Giving Tuesday,

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:55.842
so that he and his army of volunteers
can support their community

00:09:55.883 --> 00:09:58.886
in whatever way is needed all day long.

00:10:00.888 --> 00:10:03.516
And finally, this is Chloe.

00:10:04.684 --> 00:10:08.104
And Chloe is 16 now,

00:10:08.146 --> 00:10:10.523
but when she was just eight years old,

00:10:10.565 --> 00:10:12.984
she would walk to school with her mom

00:10:12.984 --> 00:10:16.320
and they would pass the same woman
without a home every day.

00:10:16.779 --> 00:10:18.698
And Chloe

00:10:18.739 --> 00:10:20.575
asked her mom a lot of questions.

00:10:21.159 --> 00:10:23.578
Starting with "Why doesn't
that woman have a home?"

00:10:23.578 --> 00:10:25.246
But also things like

00:10:25.288 --> 00:10:27.665
"What about when she needs
to use the bathroom?"

00:10:28.166 --> 00:10:30.042
"What happens when she has her period?"

00:10:31.169 --> 00:10:33.004
Chloe was learning to sew at the time,

00:10:33.004 --> 00:10:38.176
so she asked her grandma to help her
sew a beautiful tote bag,

00:10:38.176 --> 00:10:41.971
and they filled it with toiletries
and hygienic products and other goodies.

00:10:42.013 --> 00:10:43.848
And they gifted it to that woman.

00:10:43.890 --> 00:10:45.183
Not just handed it to her,

00:10:45.183 --> 00:10:47.852
but with connection and with conversation.

00:10:47.852 --> 00:10:50.646
And the woman said she couldn't
even remember the last time

00:10:50.646 --> 00:10:52.356
somebody had asked her her name.

00:10:53.524 --> 00:10:58.738
Chloe has since made and delivered
5,000 of those bags.

00:10:59.405 --> 00:11:02.116
Chloe also DM’d us on Instagram

00:11:02.116 --> 00:11:04.493
to ask if we had a Giving Tuesday Kids

00:11:04.535 --> 00:11:06.787
and if we didn't, could she start it?

00:11:06.787 --> 00:11:07.997
(Laughter)

00:11:08.039 --> 00:11:11.417
And of course -- because she's
a very entrepreneurial kid --

00:11:11.459 --> 00:11:14.295
And of course the answer was yes.

00:11:14.295 --> 00:11:16.130
And what can we do to help?

00:11:16.172 --> 00:11:17.381
And a few years later,

00:11:17.423 --> 00:11:20.718
there is a global community
of young people

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:25.264
who have performed hundreds
of thousands of acts of service,

00:11:25.264 --> 00:11:27.850
fighting bullying and hunger,

00:11:27.892 --> 00:11:30.770
and drawing attention
to mental health among young people

00:11:30.770 --> 00:11:32.688
and the importance of community.

00:11:32.688 --> 00:11:35.691
And they're connected to
and support each other.

00:11:37.735 --> 00:11:42.323
So for every Chloe and Allison and Sujan

00:11:42.365 --> 00:11:46.285
and the people with the chalk
and the people with the rocks,

00:11:46.285 --> 00:11:49.205
there are countless millions more,

00:11:49.205 --> 00:11:53.084
and the algorithms just don't put them
at the top of our feeds.

00:11:53.125 --> 00:11:54.585
But they're there.

00:11:55.169 --> 00:11:56.921
And if we pay attention,

00:11:56.921 --> 00:12:00.591
then they remind us
that ordinary acts of giving

00:12:00.591 --> 00:12:02.843
are actually extraordinary.

00:12:03.594 --> 00:12:07.181
They remind us that generosity
is not a burden.

00:12:07.181 --> 00:12:08.641
It's a gift.

00:12:08.641 --> 00:12:10.101
And it's an antidote.

00:12:10.142 --> 00:12:13.396
It's an antidote to our fear
and our anxiety,

00:12:13.437 --> 00:12:16.023
to loneliness and isolation,

00:12:16.023 --> 00:12:18.651
to outrage and indignation.

00:12:19.527 --> 00:12:22.697
And they remind us that we have this tool

00:12:22.697 --> 00:12:26.409
accessible to every single one
of us every day,

00:12:26.450 --> 00:12:29.370
which is the power to change
someone else's day,

00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:31.831
or maybe even life for the better.

00:12:32.957 --> 00:12:36.544
And when we use that tool collectively,

00:12:36.585 --> 00:12:41.173
we can build a more joyful,

00:12:41.173 --> 00:12:45.052
more equitable and more peaceful future.

00:12:45.511 --> 00:12:48.389
And that does not seem
like too radical a thing to imagine.

00:12:50.099 --> 00:12:51.267
Thank you.

00:12:51.309 --> 00:12:55.730
(Applause)

